\documentclass[11pt]{book}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\title{Tide Pool Notes}
\author{M.~Okafor}
\date{2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\chapter{Preface}
These notes grew out of five winters of low-tide walking around the
Gower coast. They are written for students who already know the common
families and want practice reading shores rather than specimens.

\tableofcontents

\chapter{Shorelines}
Rocky ledges reward patience: the same pool visited at neap tide and at
springs is two different places.
\section{Rocky Ledges}
Look first at the splash zone, then at the mid-shore gutters, and only
then at the showier anemones.
\section{Sand Flats}
Ripple marks point to the last ebb, and the shortest trails often lead
to the richest burrows.

\chapter{Finding Species}
A shore walk is a starting point, not an answer; a tentative name still
has to survive comparison with a full description.
\section{Working a Grid}
Pace the transect in order and never skip a square you cannot search
properly on this visit.

\chapter{Taking Notes}
A notebook works by accumulation, so one smudged page can send the
whole season's record down the wrong branch.
\section{Waterproof Ink}
The classic pitfall is writing up a soaked page as if it were legible.

\chapter{Closing the Season}
Cataloguing finds, label conventions, and the museum handover close the
season's work.
\end{document}

